1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,355
Two engineers report the same incident.
One writes: "CI fails with a timeout."

2
00:00:09,395 --> 00:00:14,626
The other writes: "The commit is broken."
CI is the automated system that builds and

3
00:00:14,666 --> 00:00:15,819
tests every change.

4
00:00:16,159 --> 00:00:19,915
One of those sentences is a
symptom, and the other is a guess.

5
00:00:19,955 --> 00:00:21,874
Which is which? Say it out loud.

6
00:00:26,714 --> 00:00:30,681
The first sentence is the symptom.
It says what was observed, without

7
00:00:30,721 --> 00:00:33,875
interpretation. The second
sentence is already a guess.

8
00:00:33,915 --> 00:00:38,288
It names a culprit before anybody has
looked at the state of the repository.

9
00:00:38,628 --> 00:00:43,017
Most damage in Git incidents is done by
a command typed before the state was

10
00:00:43,057 --> 00:00:47,738
understood. And that command usually follows
from a sentence like the second one.

11
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:52,459
This video gives you a fixed procedure
that keeps guesses out of your hands until

12
00:00:52,499 --> 00:00:57,121
the evidence is in. Hold on to that guess.
Its close cousin turns up later, in a

13
00:00:57,161 --> 00:00:58,228
colleague's report.

14
00:01:00,068 --> 00:01:03,803
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:04,143 --> 00:01:07,952
You've seen where a file can be:
working tree, index, repository.

16
00:01:07,992 --> 00:01:12,808
A quick reminder: the working tree is the
files you edit, the index is the list of

17
00:01:12,848 --> 00:01:17,605
what the next commit will contain, and the
repository holds the recorded commits.

18
00:01:17,945 --> 00:01:23,146
Now you get the method that this course
uses for every problem, from a missing file

19
00:01:23,186 --> 00:01:28,260
to a rewritten shared branch. It has eleven
steps and one fixed way to write down

20
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:33,438
the result, a box of seven lines. From here
on, every investigation in every video

21
00:01:33,478 --> 00:01:37,605
ends with that box. So learn it
now, while the examples are small.

22
00:01:37,945 --> 00:01:42,514
We'll run exactly one Git command in this
video, and it's one you already know: git

23
00:01:42,554 --> 00:01:45,236
status. The point is the
method, not the command.

24
00:01:47,076 --> 00:01:48,654
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:48,994 --> 00:01:50,406
After this video you can:

26
00:01:50,746 --> 00:01:53,333
List the steps of the
root-cause framework in order.

27
00:01:53,673 --> 00:01:56,963
Separate a symptom from a
hypothesis for a given report.

28
00:01:57,303 --> 00:02:01,095
Write a seven-line root-cause box
for a behavior that has been shown.

29
00:02:01,435 --> 00:02:05,282
Explain why every step up to naming
the root cause is read-only.

30
00:02:07,122 --> 00:02:10,974
Every problem in this course is
handled in the same eleven steps.

31
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:15,183
Symptom. Write down what was
observed, without interpretation.

32
00:02:15,223 --> 00:02:20,353
"CI fails with a timeout" is a symptom.
"The commit is broken" is already a guess.

33
00:02:20,693 --> 00:02:24,513
Observe. Look before you
touch. Start with git status.

34
00:02:24,853 --> 00:02:28,660
Collect evidence. Run the diagnosis
ritual and keep the output.

35
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:32,752
The ritual is ten commands, and
it's the subject of the next video.

36
00:02:33,092 --> 00:02:38,376
Understand state. State what the working
tree, the index, HEAD, the branches and the

37
00:02:38,416 --> 00:02:43,574
remote contain. The remote is the name your
repository has for another repository,

38
00:02:43,614 --> 00:02:48,898
usually the copy on the server. Say it in
words. If you can't say it, you don't have

39
00:02:48,938 --> 00:02:49,341
it yet.

40
00:02:49,681 --> 00:02:54,847
Form hypotheses. List every mechanism
that could produce the symptom from that

41
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:59,053
state. A hypothesis is a possible
explanation. Note the plural.

42
00:02:59,093 --> 00:03:01,989
One hypothesis is a
belief, not an analysis.

43
00:03:02,329 --> 00:03:07,389
Test hypotheses. For each one, name the
command whose output would differ if it were

44
00:03:07,429 --> 00:03:08,422
true, and run it.

45
00:03:08,762 --> 00:03:14,022
Identify root cause. Name the mechanism
that survived, and the layer it belongs to:

46
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:18,429
Git, GitHub or GitHub Actions. The
layer is part of every root cause.

47
00:03:18,469 --> 00:03:23,666
Remember "the merge is blocked" from video
2: three layers, three unrelated fixes.

48
00:03:24,006 --> 00:03:28,327
Select lowest-risk fix. List the
possible fixes with their risk labels.

49
00:03:28,367 --> 00:03:31,950
Choose the one that destroys
least and is simplest to undo.

50
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:35,548
Execute. Preview, then
run, one change at a time.

51
00:03:35,888 --> 00:03:38,764
Verify. Repeat the commands
that showed the problem.

52
00:03:38,804 --> 00:03:42,409
The evidence must have changed,
and for the reason you predicted.

53
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:47,703
Prevent. Change a habit, a setting or a
rule so that the problem cannot recur

54
00:03:47,743 --> 00:03:48,286
silently.

55
00:03:48,626 --> 00:03:50,610
Two rules make the framework safe.

56
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:55,632
The first rule: everything up to and
including "identify root cause" is read-only.

57
00:03:55,672 --> 00:03:58,857
Seven steps, and not one of
them changes the repository.

58
00:03:58,897 --> 00:04:03,637
The reason is the sentence from the hook:
most damage in Git incidents is done by a

59
00:04:03,677 --> 00:04:08,417
command typed before the state was understood.
If you haven't changed anything, you

60
00:04:08,457 --> 00:04:12,966
can't have made it worse, and the evidence
you collected is still the evidence.

61
00:04:13,306 --> 00:04:18,494
The second rule: every explanation ends in
the same seven-line box, so that a reader

62
00:04:18,534 --> 00:04:20,548
can check whether it is complete.

63
00:04:20,888 --> 00:04:25,443
Observed behavior: what was seen. Git
state: what the working tree, index, HEAD,

64
00:04:25,483 --> 00:04:29,062
refs and remote held. Mechanism:
what Git does with that state.

65
00:04:29,102 --> 00:04:33,542
Root cause: the one fact that, had it
been different, would have prevented the

66
00:04:33,582 --> 00:04:38,252
symptom. Why Git does this: the design
reason. Correct fix: the lowest-risk change

67
00:04:38,292 --> 00:04:42,101
that repairs the state. Prevention:
what stops a silent recurrence.

68
00:04:42,441 --> 00:04:47,239
Root cause versus mechanism. These two lines
are the ones people merge, so separate

69
00:04:47,279 --> 00:04:50,737
them now. The mechanism is what
Git does with a given state.

70
00:04:50,777 --> 00:04:54,409
It's general: it would be true
in any repository in that state.

71
00:04:54,449 --> 00:04:59,131
The root cause is one fact about this case:
the fact that, had it been different,

72
00:04:59,171 --> 00:05:02,512
would have prevented the symptom.
The mechanism is a rule.

73
00:05:02,552 --> 00:05:05,835
The root cause is the particular
thing that met the rule.

74
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:10,265
If the two still blur, that's normal.
You'll see the difference in the demo.

75
00:05:10,605 --> 00:05:15,293
When not to use it. The framework describes
what you can learn from a repository.

76
00:05:15,333 --> 00:05:19,905
The textbook is explicit about the limit
of its evidence step: the ten commands

77
00:05:19,945 --> 00:05:24,691
describe one clone. They say nothing certain
about the server until you fetch, and

78
00:05:24,731 --> 00:05:29,186
nothing at all about GitHub objects such
as pull requests, rulesets and check

79
00:05:29,226 --> 00:05:33,973
results. When the root cause is in another
layer, the framework still applies, but

80
00:05:34,013 --> 00:05:36,366
the evidence has to come from that layer.

81
00:05:38,206 --> 00:05:41,522
Think of the framework as a
line drawn across a page.

82
00:05:41,562 --> 00:05:45,005
Above the line you only read.
Below the line you write.

83
00:05:45,345 --> 00:05:49,003
Above the line are the seven
steps from symptom to root cause.

84
00:05:49,043 --> 00:05:52,582
Below it are four: select the
fix, execute, verify, prevent.

85
00:05:52,622 --> 00:05:57,116
You cross the line once, deliberately,
with a named root cause in your hand.

86
00:05:57,456 --> 00:06:02,231
The model breaks in one honest place, and
the next video takes it up: "read-only"

87
00:06:02,271 --> 00:06:06,630
describes what you intend, not everything
that every command does on disk.

88
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:11,563
git status can refresh cached file information
in the index file and write the file

89
00:06:11,603 --> 00:06:16,021
back. That never changes history. It
matters when you investigate a damaged

90
00:06:16,061 --> 00:06:19,885
repository, and there the textbook's
advice is to work on a copy.

91
00:06:21,725 --> 00:06:23,681
Here's the whole method on one page.

92
00:06:48,221 --> 00:06:52,150
Now a real case. Treat what follows
as a report from a colleague.

93
00:06:52,190 --> 00:06:56,790
They send you this piece of terminal
and one sentence: "Git is not saving my

94
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:57,279
commit."

95
00:07:00,119 --> 00:07:04,770
We run nothing else. We fill the seven
lines from what this transcript shows.

96
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:09,887
Try it now: pause, and write the first two
lines yourself, observed behavior and Git

97
00:07:09,927 --> 00:07:13,420
state. Thirty seconds, then
compare your answer with mine.

98
00:07:18,260 --> 00:07:21,916
Observed behavior. First strip the
interpretation from the report.

99
00:07:21,956 --> 00:07:26,283
"Git is not saving my commit" is a guess
about Git, a cousin of "the commit is

100
00:07:26,323 --> 00:07:30,595
broken". What was seen? git commit -m
"Describe the project" printed a status

101
00:07:30,635 --> 00:07:35,298
listing instead of a commit line, and ended
with exit status 1, the number a command

102
00:07:35,338 --> 00:07:37,818
reports when it finishes.
No commit was made.

103
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:43,507
Git state. Read it off the output. On branch
main. README.md is modified, under the

104
00:07:43,547 --> 00:07:48,701
heading "Changes not staged for commit".
The last line says "no changes added to

105
00:07:48,741 --> 00:07:53,764
commit". So after the edit, the working
tree holds the new line, and the index

106
00:07:53,804 --> 00:07:54,284
doesn't.

107
00:07:54,624 --> 00:07:59,632
Mechanism. What does Git do with that state?
You know this from video 3: git commit

108
00:07:59,672 --> 00:08:04,680
records the index. An index that equals the
last commit gives it nothing to record,

109
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:06,869
so it prints the status and refuses.

110
00:08:07,209 --> 00:08:12,009
Root cause. The one fact that, had it
been different, would have prevented the

111
00:08:12,049 --> 00:08:15,855
symptom: the change to README.md
was never added to the index.

112
00:08:16,195 --> 00:08:20,236
Compare those two lines. The mechanism
would hold in any repository.

113
00:08:20,276 --> 00:08:23,417
The root cause is about this
file in this repository.

114
00:08:23,457 --> 00:08:24,737
That's the difference.

115
00:08:25,077 --> 00:08:29,954
Why Git does this. The design reason: the
index exists so that you choose what a

116
00:08:29,994 --> 00:08:34,011
commit contains. A commit is what
was staged, not what is on disk.

117
00:08:34,351 --> 00:08:39,731
Correct fix. The lowest-risk change. Git
prints it in the transcript: use "git add

118
00:08:39,771 --> 00:08:44,820
<file>..." to update what will be
committed. Add the path, then commit again.

119
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,464
Both commands are 🟢 SAFE: they only add.

120
00:08:47,804 --> 00:08:52,798
Prevention. Read git status before each
commit, and read git diff --cached as the

121
00:08:52,838 --> 00:08:55,036
text of what you're about to record.

122
00:08:57,376 --> 00:09:01,034
Notice what did the work. We didn't
search for the error message.

123
00:09:01,074 --> 00:09:05,812
We read the output that Git printed, and
most of the box was in it: the state in the

124
00:09:05,852 --> 00:09:10,534
heading, and the fix in the hint. The
textbook's wording for this failure is short:

125
00:09:10,574 --> 00:09:15,141
exit status 1 and no commit. The change
is in the working tree, and the index was

126
00:09:15,181 --> 00:09:15,938
never updated.

127
00:09:16,278 --> 00:09:20,696
One more observation about the layer.
Which layer acted: Git, GitHub, or GitHub

128
00:09:20,736 --> 00:09:22,107
Actions? Say it out loud.

129
00:09:26,947 --> 00:09:31,803
Git. Not GitHub, and not Actions.
Nothing in this report left the laptop.

130
00:09:33,643 --> 00:09:35,291
Five mistakes to watch for.

131
00:09:35,631 --> 00:09:40,576
Writing a guess in the symptom line. Root
cause: the reporter interpreted before

132
00:09:40,616 --> 00:09:45,687
observing; "the commit is broken" names a
culprit, while "CI fails with a timeout"

133
00:09:45,727 --> 00:09:46,933
names what was seen.

134
00:09:47,273 --> 00:09:52,810
Stopping at one hypothesis. Root cause: one
hypothesis is a belief, not an analysis;

135
00:09:52,850 --> 00:09:56,661
with nothing to compare it
against, no test can reject it.

136
00:09:57,001 --> 00:10:01,683
Running a fix before the root cause is
named. Root cause: the read-only rule was

137
00:10:01,723 --> 00:10:05,933
skipped, and a state-changing command
destroyed or altered the evidence.

138
00:10:06,273 --> 00:10:10,057
Writing the mechanism twice and
calling one copy the root cause.

139
00:10:10,097 --> 00:10:15,076
Root cause: the two lines were not separated;
the mechanism is the general rule, the

140
00:10:15,116 --> 00:10:17,466
root cause is the one fact of this case.

141
00:10:17,806 --> 00:10:22,669
Leaving out the layer. Root cause: the
symptom looked the same in Git, GitHub and

142
00:10:22,709 --> 00:10:26,483
GitHub Actions, and the explanation
never said which one acted.

143
00:10:28,323 --> 00:10:33,697
Now, out of the lab. An ML platform team
has a nightly job that publishes evaluation

144
00:10:33,737 --> 00:10:37,629
results, and one morning the
published numbers are a day old.

145
00:10:37,669 --> 00:10:42,270
The first message in the incident
channel says "the pipeline is broken".

146
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:46,137
Quick quiz, two options: symptom,
or guess? Say it out loud.

147
00:10:50,977 --> 00:10:54,737
A guess. It names a culprit and
reports nothing that was seen.

148
00:10:54,777 --> 00:10:59,579
The engineer who takes the incident
rewrites it as a symptom: "The results page

149
00:10:59,619 --> 00:11:03,134
shows yesterday's run; last
night's job reported success."

150
00:11:03,174 --> 00:11:08,282
Then she lists mechanisms instead of picking
one, and for each she names the command

151
00:11:08,322 --> 00:11:13,247
or the page whose output would differ if
it were true, and which layer it belongs

152
00:11:13,287 --> 00:11:15,331
to: Git, GitHub or GitHub Actions.

153
00:11:15,671 --> 00:11:20,447
She changes nothing until one mechanism is
left. Her write-up is seven lines long,

154
00:11:20,487 --> 00:11:25,322
and the reviewer can check it line by line.
That's the whole value of a fixed form:

155
00:11:25,362 --> 00:11:26,849
a missing line is visible.

156
00:11:28,689 --> 00:11:36,418
Your turn. Do Exercise 1.3, Level 1, "the
diagnosis ritual on a small state", in

157
00:11:36,458 --> 00:11:39,622
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

158
00:11:39,962 --> 00:11:44,552
Before you run each command of the exercise,
predict what it will print for the

159
00:11:44,592 --> 00:11:48,948
state you built, and which place it
reads: working tree, index, HEAD, refs,

160
00:11:48,988 --> 00:11:52,289
configuration or remote. Write
the prediction down first.

161
00:11:54,129 --> 00:11:58,598
Q467: "Every explanation in this course
ends in the same seven-line form.

162
00:11:58,638 --> 00:12:03,355
What are the seven lines, and how does
"root cause" differ from "mechanism"?"

163
00:12:05,695 --> 00:12:10,483
Answer out loud, from memory. A strong answer
gives the seven lines in order without

164
00:12:10,523 --> 00:12:15,081
hesitation, and then distinguishes the
two lines with an example of its own, not

165
00:12:15,121 --> 00:12:19,506
with a definition recited twice. If you
can take one small failure and show a

166
00:12:19,546 --> 00:12:24,334
mechanism line that would be true in any
repository beside a root-cause line that is

167
00:12:24,374 --> 00:12:26,346
true only of this one, you have it.

168
00:12:28,186 --> 00:12:32,840
Let's land this. You should now be able
to say: a symptom is what was observed,

169
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:37,772
without interpretation. The framework has
eleven steps, from symptom to prevention,

170
00:12:37,812 --> 00:12:41,516
and everything up to and including
the root cause is read-only.

171
00:12:41,556 --> 00:12:46,151
I form more than one hypothesis and name
the command that would separate them.

172
00:12:46,191 --> 00:12:50,905
I write the result in seven lines: observed
behavior, Git state, mechanism, root

173
00:12:50,945 --> 00:12:55,599
cause, why Git does this, correct fix,
prevention. And the root cause names its

174
00:12:55,639 --> 00:12:55,956
layer.

175
00:12:57,796 --> 00:13:00,564
Read section 1.10 of Chapter 1.

176
00:13:00,904 --> 00:13:05,873
Learn the seven lines by heart; every later
video ends an investigation with them.

177
00:13:06,213 --> 00:13:10,712
Challenge: take the last Git problem
you solved by searching for a command.

178
00:13:10,752 --> 00:13:15,674
Write its root-cause box. Mark each line
you cannot fill; those lines are what the

179
00:13:15,714 --> 00:13:16,764
course will teach.

180
00:13:17,104 --> 00:13:21,146
Today you got a method that works
on a calm day and on a bad one.

181
00:13:21,186 --> 00:13:26,295
Learn the seven lines before the next video.
Next time: the ten-command diagnosis.

182
00:13:26,335 --> 00:13:29,560
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

183
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:31,068
See you in the next one.
